---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const CI = ".github/workflows/ci.yml";
const SHARDS = "scripts/ci/pytest_shards.py";

const H = {
  short: "What runs before a change can merge",
  how: "Locally, then in CI",
  example: "The oligo change, through the change script and the shards",
  rules: "Shards, the harness, guards and fuzzing",
  ref: "Targets, jobs and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Locally, a pre-commit hook checks what's staged, and <code>make check</code> runs eight
      targets: workflows, the chart, infrastructure, dependencies, the web app, this site, Python
      and Rust. In CI, a script first decides whether a change is docs only. If it isn't, every
      code job runs, Python's tests in sixteen shards, and one job, "CI passed", is the only
      required check. Inside any gate, an unexpected skip fails the run (<Adr n="0019" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      The pre-commit hook runs Ruff, mypy, the workspace imports on Python 3.12, and Prettier, on
      the files as they're staged rather than as they are on disk (<Path p="scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit" />).{" "}
      <code>make check</code> runs its targets in order, and <code>make</code> stops at the first that
      fails, so <code>make -k check</code> runs them all. It refuses the browser opt-out <code
        >IKI_SKIP_BROWSER_TESTS=1</code
      >, which is only for a partial local pytest run:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="Makefile" start="^check: check-workflows" />
    <p>
      The test VM runs a full check off the laptop. The host-side wrapper gives each run its own
      id, sets <code>IKI_GATE=1</code> there, and fetches failing browser traces back; the VM sets{" "}
      <code>IKI_VM_RUN</code>, so pytest run directly there keeps the gate's rules. The VM itself,
      and <code>vm-run.sh</code>, live outside this repository (<Path
        p="scripts/ci/with-browser-traces.sh"
      />, <Path p="CONTRIBUTING.md" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      In CI, "What changed" runs <Path p="scripts/ci/changes.sh" />, which answers two questions:
      whether the change is code, and whether it needs the server image. Docs-only changes run
      Prettier and the docs tests instead of the code jobs. Code changes run Python's lint, types
      and tests, TypeScript (lint, types, Vitest, the generated client, the build and this site),
      Rust, the server image (built natively on arm64 too, for pull requests and merge groups), the
      worker and
      plugin images, OpenTofu, dependencies and dependency review, Slurm, and the dev cluster
      (<PageLink to="The dev cluster" />). A change to a package's README can be docs to the code
      jobs and still be copied into the server image, so it still builds that image.
    </p>
    <p>
      "CI passed" fails if any job it needs is neither a success nor skipped (<Path
        p={CI}
        sym="name: CI passed"
      />). A new push to a pull request cancels its earlier run.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      The oligo Chemistry tab change from <PageLink to="Where a change is tested" hash="example" />,
      on its pull request. The change script finds code, not docs, because a plugin's files never
      count as docs; nor does anything under a <code>src/</code> directory, and a new branch or an
      unknown base runs everything:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="scripts/ci/changes.sh" start="^is_docs\(\) \{" />
    <p>
      So every code job runs. The browser test lands in a browser shard, the first-party test in a
      plain shard, and the oligo plugin's action tests in the worker shard. Each shard collects the
      whole suite, keeps its kind, and deals the files out like this:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={SHARDS} start="^def assign\(" />
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="The Python matrix" open>
        <p>
          Sixteen shards: Python 3.12, the oldest supported version, runs seven plain; Python 3.14,
          what servers run, runs five plain, one worker and three browser. Plain tests need Python,
          Postgres and Docker; worker tests also iki-worker, built with cargo; browser tests the
          built web app and three browsers. Every shard builds iki-wasm, since saves evaluate
          plugins' rules with it. Browser shards may take 45 minutes, the others 30 (<Path
            p={CI}
            sym="python-tests:"
          />).
        </p>
        <p>
          A test file is never split across shards, so its module-scoped fixtures start once. Files
          are dealt longest first to whichever shard has least so far, by the seconds each took last
          time, recorded per Python version and kind (<Path p="scripts/ci/test-durations.json" />). A
          new file's time is estimated from measured files nearby (<Path
            p={SHARDS}
            sym="def file_weights("
          />). The summary job checks that every test ran in exactly one shard, combines coverage,
          and writes a fresh durations table, which is copied in by hand when the shards drift apart
          (<Path p="scripts/ci/shard_summary.py" />). One shard can be run locally with <code
            >uv run pytest -p pytest_shards --shard plain:2/7</code
          >.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The harness, and no unexpected skips">
        <p>
          Postgres and SeaweedFS come from testcontainers unless the environment names them; xdist
          workers share one of each (<Path p="server/tests/conftest.py" />). Inside a gate (<code
            >IKI_GATE=1</code
          >, CI, or the test VM), any skip not on an allow-list fails the run, and so does a skip at
          collection (<Path p="scripts/ci/pytest_gate_skips.py" />). An <code>xfail</code> that passes
          fails too (<Path p="pyproject.toml" sym="xfail_strict = true" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Generated files are checked against what generates them">
        <p>
          The first-party plugins' trees (<Path p="spec/tests/test_first_party.py" />), the core
          plugin's generated app and migrations (<Path
            p="server/tests/test_core_plugin.py"
            sym="def test_the_generated_app_matches_the_manifests("
          />), the bundled plugin components (<code>pnpm test</code>), the OpenAPI document and the
          clients made from it (<PageLink to="Packages and the SDK" />), and the rule that no <code
            >.wasm</code
          > is ever in git (<PageLink to="WebAssembly" />) (<Adr n={[38, 114, 191]} />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Authorization fuzzing">
        <p>
          The access matrix lists every API operation. The IDOR tests send each one as a project's
          Admin with another project's objects swapped in, one identifier at a time and all at once;
          each must answer exactly as it does for an identifier that names nothing (<Adr n="0101" />,{" "}
          <Path p="server/tests/test_api_idor.py" sym="def test_another_projects_objects_are_never_reached(" />).
          Every worker job route is fuzzed the same way with another worker's job (<Path
            p="server/tests/test_worker_idor.py"
            sym="def test_every_job_route_is_fuzzed("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="What this repository doesn't hold">
        <p>
          The test VM's machine and its settings, the lock that keeps heavy local runs to two at a
          time, and the split between a scoped gate for changes local to one page and the full gate
          for shared ones are working practice, kept outside the repository. Nothing here encodes
          them, so this page can't cite them. In the repository, CI's only scoping is docs versus
          code, and whether the server image is needed.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the docs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            The Makefile's first line and <Adr n="0019" /> say <code>make check</code> runs exactly
            what CI runs. It doesn't run the dev cluster, the image builds, Slurm, dependency review
            or the docs job, and runs one Python version where CI runs two.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Make targets</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><code>make check-python</code>, <code>check-web</code>, <code>check-rust</code>, <code>check-architecture</code> <Path p="Makefile" /></li>
        <li><code>make check-image</code>, <code>check-slurm</code>: what <code>make check</code> leaves out</li>
        <li><code>make dev-ci</code>: the dev cluster, end to end, then the restore test</li>
        <li><code>make live-due</code>, <code>check-gcp-live</code>, <code>check-aws-live</code>: <PageLink to="Live clouds and scale" /></li>
        <li><code>make core</code>, <code>make first-party</code>, <code>make client</code>: regenerate what the guards check</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Workflows</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={CI} />: every pull request, merge group and push to main</li>
        <li><Path p=".github/workflows/publish.yml" />, <Path p=".github/workflows/release.yml" />: <PageLink to="Releases and images" /></li>
        <li><Path p=".github/workflows/scale.yml" />: nightly load tests</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0019" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0101" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
